# Copy to `.env` for local development:  cp .env.example .env
#
# Why this exists: EXPO_PUBLIC_* values are inlined into the JS bundle when it
# is built. `expo start` and `eas update` both read `.env` from the project
# root, so without this file transport fails with "Transport is not available
# in this version of the app" — including in a published OTA update.
#
# None of these are secrets: anything prefixed EXPO_PUBLIC_ is readable out of
# a shipped bundle. Real secrets belong on a server.
#
# Keep in sync with eas.json → build.<profile>.env.

# ── UniGo transport (partner API) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Publishable partner key. Scoped so it is safe to ship: it can browse open
# trips, create pending bookings, start payment and read back its own bookings.
# It cannot hold a seat without paying, and cannot see other partners' data.
# Reissue with: cd backend/unigo-backend && npm run partner:create -- --slug hostelhubb --rotate
EXPO_PUBLIC_UNIGO_API_URL=https://yenko-backend.onrender.com
EXPO_PUBLIC_UNIGO_PARTNER_KEY=pk_live_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
